<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kyawswa I of Pinya</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1344">1344</a>–<a href="/years/1350">1350</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kyawswa I was king of Pinya in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> from 1344 to 1350, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thihathu">Thihathu</a>, founder of the kingdom. His reign fell in a period of rivalry between Pinya and the breakaway kingdom of Sagaing, ruled by his half-brother's line. He was succeeded by his sons, and Pinya was sacked by Shan forces in 1364, after which Ava replaced it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kyawswa-i-of-pinya.jpg" alt="Kyawswa I of Pinya" width="320" height="469" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sir Richard Carnac Temple · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ngazishin_Nat.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Kyawswa was born in 1299 at Myinsaing, the elder son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thihathu">Thihathu</a>, co-regent of Myinsaing, and Queen Mi Saw U of Pagan, and grew up at the Pinle Palace. Because his stepbrother Uzana was of Pagan royalty on both sides, Thihathu ranked Uzana first in the succession and Kyawswa second. On 7 February 1313 Kyawswa was made governor of Pinle, and by 1315 he commanded troops of his own. In 1316–17 he failed to retake Sagaing from his half-brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saw-yun">Saw Yun</a>, but with Uzana he forced Thawun Nge, ruler of Toungoo, to submit in 1317.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thihathu">Thihathu</a> died in 1325 the kingdom split between Uzana's Pinya and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saw-yun">Saw Yun</a>'s Sagaing. Kyawswa kept his own army and policy and ordered his commander Khin Nyo to assassinate Saw Yun, an attempt that failed. The brothers' rivalry left Pinya's southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> to fend for themselves against attacks on Prome in 1330 and Thayet in 1334. In 1340 Kyawswa refused to hand over five white elephants, symbols of kingship, and sent two ordinary ones instead. Uzana gave up power on 1 September 1340 and became a monk; Kyawswa was claiming the title of king by 1342 and was undisputed ruler of Pinya from 29 March 1344.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Known as Nga-zi Shin, Lord of the Five White Elephants, he secured the core region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyaukse">Kyaukse</a> through bribery, flattery and threat, appointing his brother Nawrahta governor of Pinle, though Toungoo remained beyond his control. A keen horseman, he formed elite cavalry and shielded infantry and joined their military dances. He made his second son, Kyawswa II, heir, and Nawrahta defected to Sagaing in 1349. He built the Lay-Myet-Hna Pagada and was an influential poet, whose Myinsaing Shwepyi is the best-known poem in the kagyin form. He died on 12 December 1350 and is venerated as Nga-zi Shin Nat, one of the Outer Thirty Seven Spirits.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kyawswa I of Pinya” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Uzana I and <b>Kyawswa I</b> were among its kings.</blockquote><a href="/state/pinya-kingdom">Pinya Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Pinle, in what is now <a href="/countries/myanmar">Myanmar</a>. Died in Pinya, in what is now <a href="/countries/myanmar">Myanmar</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=21.5,96&amp;year=1347" aria-label="Pinle on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Kyawswa I of Pinya"><image href="/maps/globes/g090n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5477 5712h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5477 5712h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=21.5,96&amp;year=1347">What was here in 1347? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
